\section{Importing a PDF the user holds}
\label{sec:pdf-import}

Some PDFs scholar cannot fetch.
A publisher may answer the download with a bot-check page, and that is
where scholar stops (\cref{ch:network}); the user may then get the PDF
by other means they are entitled to, a browser on the library's network
for instance.
Without a way to hand that file over, [[scholar pdf quote]] and the
full-text classification (which read the PDF through [[get_pdf]]) never
see it, the next session does not find it either, and the full text
behind a quoted passage lives outside scholar.
[[import_pdf]] closes the gap: it checks that the file is a PDF, copies
it into the cache under the key a later lookup for that URL or DOI
uses, and writes a record of what file it was.

The rest of this section takes the parts in the order an import meets
them: which key the copy goes under (\cref{sec:pdf-import-key}), how
the file is told from a page that is not a PDF
(\cref{sec:pdf-import-check}), and how it is written, recorded and
protected against overwriting (\cref{sec:pdf-import-write}).
How the copies are kept by [[clear_pdf_cache]] and counted by
[[get_pdf_cache_info]] was shown with those functions
(\cref{sec:pdf-cache-dir}); the lookups that find an imported PDF by
DOI without a request are in [[download_doi_pdf]] and [[get_pdf]]
(\cref{sec:pdf-download-doi}).

\subsection{The key of an imported PDF}
\label{sec:pdf-import-key}

By URL there is nothing to decide: [[download_pdf]] looks for
[[get_cached_pdf_path(url)]], so an import by URL goes there, and
[[scholar pdf open]] or [[pdf quote]] of that URL, and a paper whose
[[pdf_url]] it is, find it as a cache hit.

A DOI has no such key.
The download resolves the DOI to candidate URLs first
(\cref{sec:doi-resolution}) and caches the PDF under whichever URL
delivered, so the file's key depends on the answers of Unpaywall,
Semantic Scholar and the publisher at download time.
Keying an import by one of those URLs would need those requests at
import time, and the lookup would find it only while the resolvers kept
answering the same.
We considered an index file beside the cache, DOI to cached file,
consulted before resolving; it works, but it is a second structure
that must agree with the files (an entry can outlive its file, and two
imports at once must not tear the index).
Instead an import by DOI goes under the key of the DOI's resolver URL,
[[https://doi.org/<doi>]], computed from the DOI alone, and the DOI
lookups consult that key before resolving ([[download_doi_pdf]],
[[get_pdf]]).
The lookup is one [[exists]] call, and the import is found whichever URL
the resolvers would have given.
No download writes under that key through [[get_pdf]], which sends any
[[doi.org]] URL down the DOI path, so the key is the import's own slot.
The DOI is normalised as paper ids normalise it
([[Paper._normalize_doi]]: lower case, no resolver prefix), so
[[10.1000/ABC]], [[doi:10.1000/abc]] and
[[https://doi.org/10.1000/abc]] name one file.

<<pdf cache functions>>=
def get_cached_doi_pdf_path(doi: str) -> Path:
    """
    Get the cache path of a PDF imported for `doi`.

    The key is the DOI's resolver URL (`https://doi.org/` plus the
    normalised DOI), so the path follows from the DOI alone.
    """
    from scholar.scholar import Paper

    return get_cached_pdf_path(DOI_RESOLVER_URL + Paper._normalize_doi(doi))
@

\subsection{Telling a PDF from a page}
\label{sec:pdf-import-check}

[[download_pdf]] tells a PDF from an HTML page by the response's
[[Content-Type]] header; it never looks at the bytes.
A local file has no such header, and guessing one from the file name
would accept exactly the case an import must refuse: the bot-check page
saved as [[paper.pdf]].
So the import asks the question that matters for what follows: can
PDFium, the engine [[extract_pdf_pages]] reads the full text with
(\cref{sec:quote-extraction}), open the file, and does it have a page?
A file that passes is one [[pdf quote]] and the classification can
read.
When PDFium refuses a file that begins like an HTML page, the reason
says so, since that is the likely mistake: saving the publisher's
answer instead of the PDF.

<<pdf import functions>>=
def pdf_file_problem(data: bytes) -> str | None:
    """
    Return why `data` is not a readable PDF, or None if it is one.

    Readable means that PDFium (the engine `extract_pdf_pages` uses)
    opens it and finds at least one page.
    """
    import pypdfium2 as pdfium

    try:
        document = pdfium.PdfDocument(data)
    except pdfium.PdfiumError as error:
        reason = f"PDFium cannot read it ({error})"
        if looks_like_html(data):
            reason += (
                "; it begins like an HTML page, such as a login or "
                "bot-check page"
            )
        return reason
    try:
        if len(document) == 0:
            return "it has no pages"
    finally:
        document.close()
    return None


def looks_like_html(data: bytes) -> bool:
    """Whether `data` begins like an HTML document."""
    head = data[:HTML_SNIFF_BYTES].lstrip(b"\xef\xbb\xbf \t\r\n").lower()
    return head.startswith((b"<!doctype html", b"<html")) or b"<html" in head
<<pdf constants>>=
HTML_SNIFF_BYTES = 1024
@

\subsection{Copying, recording and refusing to overwrite}
\label{sec:pdf-import-write}

The file is read once into memory: the check, the hash and the copy
all need its bytes, and reading it once means that the bytes checked
are the bytes copied, even if the user's file changes meanwhile.
The user's file is only read, never moved or changed.

Each import has a record beside the copy, [[<hash>.import.json]]: the
SHA-256 and size of the content, the time of the import (UTC), the
file's base name, the [[--from]] text that says where it came from, and
the URL or DOI it was imported for, as given.
JSON of built-in types, so any later version can read it.
Only the base name is stored, not the directory the user typed: a path
says more about the user than the record needs.
The record is what marks the copy as imported, for [[clear_pdf_cache]]
and [[get_pdf_cache_info]] (\cref{sec:pdf-cache-dir}).

An existing copy under the key with other content is not overwritten
unless the caller says [[replace]]: it may be a download that works,
or an earlier import whose source is gone.
The error names the hash that is there, so the user can tell which
file it was.
The same content is not copied again; if that copy has no record yet
(a download of the same file), the record is added, since the user has
now said that this copy matters.

Two processes importing for the same key at once would each find no
copy, each write, and leave whichever finished last.
Each file is written with [[atomic_write_bytes]] (a temporary file
renamed into place, \cref{sec:atomic-write}), so no reader ever sees
half a PDF, and the whole compare-and-write runs under [[file_lock]] on
the copy's path (\cref{sec:file-lock}), so the second import sees the
first one's copy and is refused or recognised as the same.
The record is written before the PDF.
If the process dies between the two, what is left is a record without
a PDF, which marks nothing; the other order could leave an imported PDF
without its record, which the next [[clear_pdf_cache]] would delete as
a download.

